From 08788aa803fcec8ba0aafb5be3adf6ad01f72dea Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Daniel Boyd <danieljboyd@icloud.com>
Date: Fri, 9 Oct 2026 14:54:06 -0500
Subject: [PATCH 02/14] win32: let the gui draw a titled window's caption

A theme could recolour Windows' caption (DWM attributes) but not draw in
it, so apps couldn't have a caption like WinUI's (tabs, a search box,
its 46x32 caption buttons), and GSBackHandlesWindowDecorations = NO
gives borderless WS_POPUP windows without Windows' shadow, Snap, Snap
Layouts or system menu.

With the GSBackClientDrawsCaptions default (or -setClientDrawsCaptions:)
a titled window keeps its frame but has no caption of Windows': its
client area reaches the window's top (WM_NCCALCSIZE; a maximized
window's starts below its off-screen frame), and -styleoffsets gives no
top offset, so the gui's frames, events and backing store agree.  The
gui declares the parts of the caption row it drew
(-setCaptionRegionRects:kinds:count:forWindow:): the parts that drag
the window, and its minimize, zoom and close buttons.  WM_NCHITTEST
answers from them, after Windows' borders and a top resizing band, so
Windows moves, maximizes (double click) and shows the system menu (right
click) as with its own caption, and shows Snap Layouts over the zoom
button.  Mouse messages over the caption reach the gui as client-area
events, so its caption buttons light up and are clicked like its other
buttons, and Windows draws none of its own over them.

WM_SETCURSOR now leaves the cursor to Windows outside the client area,
so the resizing arrows show over the top band and borders.

AI assistance: this change and its description were written with Claude
(Anthropic's Claude Code).

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
Claude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01CGid3oWi1NnPNi1hAiK9zW
